WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:07.053
Why does a nerve terminal release more packets when there is more calcium?

2
00:00:07.683 --> 00:00:08.843
Start with the machinery.

3
00:00:09.803 --> 00:00:14.123
The action potential opens voltage-gated calcium channels in the terminal.

4
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:17.327
Calcium flows in, down a steep gradient.

5
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.650
Nearby vesicles are already docked and primed, ready to fuse.

6
00:00:22.283 --> 00:00:25.383
Calcium binds a sensor protein, synaptotagmin.

7
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:28.707
That triggers the SNARE proteins to finish zipping.

8
00:00:29.507 --> 00:00:32.027
They pull the vesicle and the membrane into one.

9
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.107
Release needs calcium outside the terminal.

10
00:00:36.127 --> 00:00:37.347
Magnesium opposes it.

11
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:43.050
Dodge and Rahamimoff measured how strongly, at the frog junction, in 1967.

12
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:49.160
They changed calcium step by step and plotted the response against calcium, both on

13
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.160
log scales.

14
00:00:50.433 --> 00:00:53.553
At low calcium, the points fall on a steep straight line.

15
00:00:54.413 --> 00:00:55.513
Its slope is near four.

16
00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:59.547
As calcium nears its normal level, the line bends over.

17
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:04.930
A slope near four means release rises roughly as the fourth power of calcium.

18
00:01:05.583 --> 00:01:07.663
On the steep part, double the calcium.

19
00:01:08.563 --> 00:01:10.163
How much more transmitter comes out?

20
00:01:15.683 --> 00:01:16.923
Far more than double.

21
00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:20.423
With their measured slope, about fourteen times more.

22
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:25.203
That is why a modest loss of calcium entry can cut release sharply.

23
00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:27.827
Why so steep, and why does it bend?

24
00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:30.707
Picture four seats that must all be filled.

25
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:36.077
In this picture, release at a site needs all four seats holding calcium at once.

26
00:01:36.617 --> 00:01:39.257
At low calcium, each seat is rarely filled.

27
00:01:40.157 --> 00:01:41.657
All four at once is rarer still.

28
00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:45.347
So release grows very steeply as calcium rises.

29
00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:48.807
On log scales, that is a straight line of slope four.

30
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.280
As calcium rises further, the seats fill up.

31
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.400
Adding more calcium cannot help much, so the line bends over.

32
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.860
A model like this fits the slope.

33
00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.060
Adding magnesium lowered the response without changing the slope.

34
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.190
That fits magnesium competing with calcium for the same seats.

35
00:02:07.833 --> 00:02:10.033
But the slope does not count binding sites.

36
00:02:10.993 --> 00:02:14.093
Saturation and calcium spread in the terminal can shape it.

37
00:02:14.617 --> 00:02:19.357
Treat four calcium ions as a reading of one curve, not a molecular fact.

38
00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.230
So calcium is a steep dial on the count.

39
00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.810
Lose some calcium entry, and you lose many packets.
